         </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="guide-body">

         <!-- ══ SECTION 1 ══ -->
         <h2 id="what-is-cse">What is a CSE meeting?</h2>

         <p>CSE stands for <strong>Committee on Special Education</strong>. It's a team — made up of you, school staff, and specialists — that meets to decide what special education services your child is entitled to receive under New York State law.</p>

         <p>The result of a CSE meeting is your child's <strong>Individualized Education Program (IEP)</strong> — a legally binding document that spells out exactly what services the district must provide: the type and frequency of instruction, therapies like speech or OT, testing accommodations, and classroom supports. Once the IEP is signed, the district is legally obligated to deliver everything in it.</p>

         <p>CSE meetings happen at several points:</p>

         <ul class="card-list">
            <li><strong>Initial eligibility meeting</strong> After the district finishes evaluating your child, the CSE meets to decide if they qualify for special education services under one of the 13 disability classifications.</li>
            <li><strong>Annual review</strong> Every year, the CSE must meet to review your child's progress and write a new IEP for the coming school year.</li>
            <li><strong>Triennial reevaluation</strong> Every three years, the district must conduct updated evaluations and reconvene the full CSE.</li>
            <li><strong>Amendment meeting</strong> Any time during the year that you or the school believes the IEP needs to change — for example, if your child is not making progress or their needs have shifted.</li>
         </ul>

         <div class="callout callout-blue">
            <div class="callout-title">CSE vs. CPSE</div>
            The CSE handles children ages 5 through 21 (school-age). If your child is under 5, they fall under the <strong>Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE)</strong>. The "Turning 5" transition from CPSE to CSE is a separate process with its own timeline and is often where services get reduced — <a href="/guides/cpse-preschool-special-education/">see our CPSE guide</a> for more on that transition.
         </div>

         <!-- ══ SECTION 2 ══ -->
         <h2 id="who-attends">Who must attend the CSE meeting</h2>

         <p>New York State law (8 NYCRR § 200.3) is very specific about who must be at the table. An improperly staffed meeting can make the resulting IEP legally invalid — so it's worth knowing this list and checking it before you walk in the door.</p>

         <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
               <tr>
                  <th>Member</th>
                  <th>Required?</th>
                  <th>Their role</th>
               </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>You (the parent)</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required</td>
                  <td>You are an equal member of this team — not a guest. Your observations about your child carry legal weight.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>District Representative</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required</td>
                  <td>Must have authority to commit district money and resources. If this person can't say yes to services on the spot, that's a problem.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>Special Education Teacher</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required</td>
                  <td>Advises on instruction methods, modifications, and what the child needs to access learning.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>General Education Teacher</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required if child is (or may be) in gen-ed</td>
                  <td>Explains what the mainstream classroom demands and what supports would realistically work there.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>School Psychologist</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required for initial & triennial meetings</td>
                  <td>Interprets psychological and academic testing; translates scores into educational recommendations.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>Someone who can interpret evaluations</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required</td>
                  <td>Often the psychologist fills this role — someone must explain what the test results actually mean for your child's learning.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>The student</strong></td>
                  <td class="required">Required at age 15+ for transition planning</td>
                  <td>Legally required once transition services are being discussed. You can invite your child at any age if helpful.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>Additional Parent Member</strong></td>
                  <td class="conditional">Conditional</td>
                  <td>A parent of another child with a disability, there to support you. Must be requested in writing at least 72 hours before the meeting. You can also decline this role if you prefer privacy.</td>
               </tr>
               <tr>
                  <td><strong>School Physician</strong></td>
                  <td class="conditional">Conditional</td>
                  <td>Only required if you or another member formally requests their attendance 72 hours in advance.</td>
               </tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>

         <div class="callout callout-gold">
            <div class="callout-title">On member excusals</div>
            Districts frequently ask to excuse required members due to scheduling conflicts. This is only legal if you agree <em>in writing</em>, and the excused member must submit written input on the IEP at least <strong>5 calendar days before the meeting</strong>. Never agree to excuse the psychologist or special education teacher verbally — get it in writing and review their input before you walk in.
         </div>

         <!-- ══ SECTION 3 ══ -->
         <h2 id="prepare">How to prepare for your CSE meeting</h2>

         <p>The families who get the most from CSE meetings are the ones who show up prepared. Here's what to do in the two weeks before your meeting.</p>

         <ul class="card-list checklist">
            <li><strong>Request documents 5 business days before the meeting.</strong> You have a legal right to all evaluations, reports, and the draft IEP at least 5 school days before the meeting. Send a written request (email is fine) to the CSE Chairperson. Don't let the district hand you a stack of papers at the table and ask you to decide on the spot.</li>
            <li><strong>Review last year's goals and progress reports.</strong> Pull out the current IEP and check which annual goals your child actually met. If goals were consistently missed, you need to ask at the meeting: why didn't the current approach work, and what is specifically changing this year?</li>
            <li><strong>Write a Parent Concerns Statement.</strong> A one-page summary of your observations — your child's strengths, struggles, what you're seeing at home, what you want the school year to look like. Submit it to the Chairperson before the meeting. It goes in the official record and forces the committee to address your priorities.</li>
            <li><strong>Bring any private evaluations.</strong> Reports from outside neuropsychologists, therapists, or tutors must be considered by the CSE. They are not required to follow every recommendation, but they can't ignore the data.</li>
            <li><strong>Bring a support person.</strong> A friend, family member, advocate, or attorney can sit with you — to take notes, offer a second perspective, and keep the meeting from feeling one-sided.</li>
            <li><strong>Know your right to an IEE.</strong> If you disagree with the district's evaluations, you can request an <a href="/guides/evaluation-request-ny/">Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE)</a> at the district's expense. The district either funds it or files for a hearing to defend their own evaluation.</li>
         </ul>

         <!-- INLINE CTA -->
         <div class="inline-cta">
            <div class="inline-cta-text">
               <h3>Don't know what to write to the CSE?</h3>
               <p>Our AI Letter Writer generates a custom, legally-grounded letter for your specific situation in minutes.</p>
            </div>
