         <!-- ══ SECTION 4 ══ -->
         <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights during the meeting</h2>

         <p>You are an equal member of this committee. That phrase appears in the statute for a reason — use it. Here are the specific rights you need to know before you sit down.</p>

         <ul class="card-list">
            <li><strong>Right to a language interpreter.</strong> If English is not your primary language, the district must provide interpretation services. Request this in advance when you confirm the meeting.</li>
            <li><strong>Right to audio record the meeting.</strong> In New York State, you can record the CSE meeting — but you must notify the district in writing at least 24 hours beforehand. A recording is your most reliable protection if there's a dispute later about what was agreed.</li>
            <li><strong>Right to question every recommendation.</strong> Nothing in the IEP should be presented as non-negotiable. Ask why. Ask what alternatives were considered. Ask for the data behind every service frequency and placement recommendation.</li>
            <li><strong>Right to disagree in writing.</strong> If you don't agree with what the IEP says, you can note your specific objections in writing. Your dissent becomes part of the official record and preserves your right to challenge the IEP later.</li>
            <li><strong>Right to adjourn.</strong> If you feel rushed, overwhelmed, or ambushed with new information, stop the meeting. You have the right to request an adjournment to review materials and consult with an advocate or attorney. Use it.</li>
            <li><strong>You do not have to sign the IEP that day.</strong> Take the document home. Read it carefully. A parent who signs the same day rarely has time to catch what's missing.</li>
         </ul>

         <!-- ══ SECTION 5 ══ -->
         <h2 id="iep-sections">Reading the IEP document</h2>

         <p>The IEP can run 15–30 pages. Here are the sections that matter most and what to look for in each one.</p>

         <ul class="card-list">
            <li>
               <strong>Present Levels of Academic Achievement and Functional Performance (PLAAFP)</strong>
               This is the foundation of the entire IEP — a description of where your child is right now. Every goal and service recommendation should flow from this section. Watch for: vague language ("struggles with reading"), missing data, or a PLAAFP that hasn't changed from last year despite your child's growth.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>Annual Goals</strong>
               Goals must be SMART: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound. A goal like "will improve reading skills" is not a goal — it has no baseline, no target, and no way to measure progress. Push for goals that name a specific skill, a measurable standard (e.g., "with 80% accuracy across 3 consecutive trials"), and a method of measurement.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>Special Education Services</strong>
               The exact type of instruction (resource room, ICT, self-contained), how many minutes per week, and who delivers it. Check that the frequency actually matches what you discussed in the meeting — drafts are sometimes written before the meeting and don't reflect what was agreed.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>Related Services</strong>
               Speech, OT, PT, counseling — each one lists the frequency and duration. "1x30 per week" means once per week for 30 minutes. Make sure this matches what your child's private providers recommend if you have outside evaluations.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>Accommodations vs. Modifications</strong>
               These are fundamentally different. <em>Accommodations</em> change how your child learns (extended time, text-to-speech, preferential seating) without changing what they're expected to master. <em>Modifications</em> change the academic standard itself (fewer questions, lower-grade materials). Make sure the IEP clearly separates these and that modifications are only used when genuinely necessary.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>Least Restrictive Environment (LRE) Justification</strong>
               If the CSE recommends removing your child from a general education classroom, that decision requires a written justification explaining why less restrictive options were considered and found insufficient. If this section is blank or generic, challenge it.
            </li>
            <li>
               <strong>Transition Plan (age 15+)</strong>
               For students 15 and older, the IEP must include measurable post-secondary goals for employment, education or training, and independent living. These goals must reflect your child's actual interests and be supported by vocational assessments — not be generic placeholders.
            </li>
         </ul>

         <!-- ══ SECTION 6 ══ -->
         <h2 id="after">After the meeting: the 10-day rule</h2>

         <p>Once the IEP is finalized, the district must begin implementing all services within <strong>10 school days</strong>. Not 10 business days, not when it's convenient — 10 school days.</p>

         <p>Every teacher and service provider who works with your child must be given a copy of the IEP and told specifically what their responsibilities are. If your child's speech therapist tells you in October they've never seen the IEP, that's a compliance violation.</p>

         <p>What to do if services don't start on time:</p>

         <ul class="card-list">
            <li><strong>Document it.</strong> Send an email to the CSE Chairperson noting the date services were supposed to start and asking for a written update on the delay. Emails create a paper trail.</li>
            <li><strong>File a state complaint.</strong> If the district is in clear violation of the IEP's timelines or service delivery, you can file a complaint directly with NYSED. NYSED investigates and can order the district to take corrective action.</li>
            <li><strong>Request compensatory services.</strong> If your child missed weeks of speech or OT due to the district's failure to implement the IEP, you may be entitled to make-up sessions — called compensatory education — as a remedy.</li>
         </ul>

         <!-- ══ SECTION 7 ══ -->
         <h2 id="red-flags">Red flags: signs your meeting is off track</h2>

         <p>These situations don't always mean the district is acting in bad faith — but each one is a signal to slow down, ask questions, and document what's happening.</p>

         <ul class="card-list redflag">
            <li><strong>The IEP was clearly written before the meeting.</strong> If the document lands in front of you fully completed before anyone has spoken, meaningful participation has already been bypassed. This is a procedural violation under IDEA.</li>
            <li><strong>The meeting is over in under 20 minutes.</strong> There is no legal time minimum, but a meeting covering evaluations, goals, placement, and services for a child with complex needs cannot be done in 15 minutes. Speed is a sign the outcome was predetermined.</li>
            <li><strong>You weren't given documents in advance.</strong> If you're seeing evaluations for the first time at the table, request an adjournment immediately. You cannot meaningfully participate in a meeting about data you haven't had time to read.</li>
            <li><strong>Someone is pressuring you to sign.</strong> "We just need your signature to get services started" is not a reason to sign a document you haven't fully reviewed. Signing the IEP starts the district's implementation clock — but you have the right to take it home first.</li>
            <li><strong>The Parent Member is absent with no explanation.</strong> If you requested a Parent Member and they simply didn't show up, ask the Chairperson to document the absence and reschedule if needed.</li>
            <li><strong>The District Rep says they can't commit resources.</strong> The person across from you must have actual authority. If they say "I'll have to check with my supervisor on that service," the committee is not properly constituted for that decision.</li>
         </ul>

         <!-- ══ SECTION 8 ══ -->
         <h2 id="nyc">NYC-specific: SBST vs. the regional CSE office</h2>

         <p>New York City's size creates a split in how the CSE system operates that trips up a lot of families. The key question is: <em>where does your child go to school?</em></p>

         <h3>If your child attends a traditional NYC public school</h3>
         <p>The CSE functions are handled by the <strong>School-Based Support Team (SBST)</strong> located inside your child's building. The SBST — typically a school psychologist, social worker, and educational evaluator — manages all evaluations, annual reviews, and IEP meetings. Your contact for any referral or meeting request is the SBST coordinator at your child's school.</p>

         <h3>If your child attends a private, charter, religious, or homeschool</h3>
         <p>Your contact is the <strong>regional CSE office</strong> for your geographic district. The NYC DOE runs 11 regional offices across the city. For children in non-public settings, the CSE does not write a standard IEP — instead they write an <strong>IESP (Individualized Education Services Program)</strong>, which covers related services like speech and OT but does not cover a full educational placement.</p>

         <div class="callout callout-red">
            <div class="callout-title">Critical NYC deadline: Notice of Intent</div>
            If your child is in a private school or homeschooled and you want to maintain IESP services for the next school year, you must submit a written <strong>Notice of Intent to your regional CSE office by June 1st</strong>. Missing this deadline can result in losing all services for the entire following school year. Set a calendar reminder now.
         </div>

